]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":247,"text":"…melody goes, the decade was largely marked by the gigantic shadow of Boston's Stephen Merritt (14). His multi-faceted career began under the moniker Magnetic Fields as a humble amateur of pop music who vented his fear and nostalgy via formally impeccable melodies and arrangements. The formative Distant Plastic…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":300,"text":"…This Is Not A Dream (Majora, 1992) 19. Lycia: The Burning Circle And Then Dust (Projekt) 20. Mark Lanegan: Whiskey For The Holy Ghost (SubPop) 21. Magnetic Fields: The Charm Of The Highway Strip (Merge) 22. Stereolab: Transient Random Noise Bursts With Announcements (Elektra) 23. Lightwave: Mundus Subterraneus…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":328,"text":"…in 1994 include: Rather Interesting ？ Other significant albums of the year: Lisa Germano's Geek The Girl, Nine Inch Nails's The Downward Spiral, Magnetic Fields's The Charm Of The Highway Strip, Aurora's Dimension Gate, Von Lmo's Cosmic Interception, Jon Spencer Blues Explosion's Orange, Today Is The Day's…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Recommended Tweetpop-Adjacent Band","body":"The Magnetic Fields are listed as a band \"worth a look\" for fans interested in Tweetpop, suggesting that their musical style or thematic elements align with the genre's appeal, which includes being shy, introspective, and finding loud, aggressive music unappealing. \"Tulu Lagash, Honey Bunch, and Magnetic Fields are also worth a look.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Twee Pop: A History","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jhgZrwtYE2g"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":243304366426680,"type":"album","name":"The Wayward Bus","list_id":82075953765266,"list":"RYM Top — Pop — Indie Pop","src":"musthea